Durarara!! AU
Chapter 33 – Part 1
The building looked the same as it had that morning.
Curtis came up the last stretch of sidewalk with no Raira jacket, white shirt still whole, braids a little wind-messy from an alley he was not going to name. The Tech Rider was still a closet away. The mask was still a decision in his pocket. Namie Yagiri was a clock he had not put on anyone else’s wrist.
He saw them before he reached the glass.
Rio on the step like she had claimed it. Anri a half-pace off her shoulder, bag strap in both hands, looking at the door the way she looked at things she was afraid to want.
Curtis stopped.
“Woah. How long have you both been here?”
Rio’s head snapped up.
The look she gave him inventoried the missing coat first. Not blood. Not a new hole in the shirt. Just the official color gone, like he had decided school was optional after last period.
“Long enough,” she said. “You texted sorry and then the city ate you.”
Anri’s eyes flicked from his face to the empty place where the ripped jacket should have been.
“…You’re okay.” It came out like a check, not a question. Then, smaller: “Your coat—”
“Bin,” Curtis said. “It was already a receipt. Shirt’s fine.”
Rio’s jaw eased a fraction and did not forgive him for it.
“Good. Because I told you he better not stab you again, and I meant it.” She stood, blocking the step without quite meaning to. “Anri-chan dragged me off that sidewalk because you asked. I walked. I hated it. Then we ended up here because sorry, I’ll make it up to you is not a location and I don’t trust Izaya with your geography.”
Anri’s ears went warm.
“I didn’t know if you would come home. The… voice stopped. You said thank you. Then you were gone.” She looked at him properly, the gate still sitting behind her eyes. “I was going to tell you something. I didn’t get to.”
Curtis’s mouth ticked. He knew which file she meant. Freak. Crowd. Takeshi. The stare he had walked away from.
He did not open the alley. He did not open Mikado’s busted door. He did not open Mika’s window or a hit on a last name.
“How long,” he asked again, gentler.
Rio glanced at her phone.
“Forty minutes. Maybe more. Anri-chan wouldn’t let me go back to the gate. I wouldn’t let her wait at the station like bait.” A beat. “Your building was the only place that made sense if you were still in one piece.”
Anri nodded once.
“I have pieces,” she said, careful, for him more than for Rio. “I know you asked me to take her. I know why I wasn’t allowed to look. I know you found someone who should not be a rumor.” Her fingers tightened on the strap. “I don’t know the rest. I’m not asking on the step.”
Rio’s eyes narrowed.
“See. That’s the face. That’s the I have a city in my mouth and I’m not sharing face.” She pointed at his missing coat. “You can keep the loop. I’m out of it. Fine. But you don’t get to send us away, text an IOU, and then look surprised we used our feet.”
Curtis exhaled through his nose.
“Fair.” He tipped his chin at the door. “You’re both cold. Come in. I’m not doing a briefing on the sidewalk.”
Rio did not move yet.
“Are you hurt.”
“No.”
“Are you about to disappear into a mask and a night again.”
Curtis’s mismatched eyes flicked past them, toward the closet upstairs, then back.
“…I’m going to change. That’s all I’m saying right now.”
Anri heard the gap. She did not fill it with Mika. She did not fill it with Yagiri. She only stood there with the unpaid sentence still in her chest and a boy who thought a crowd’s stare had been her verdict.
“Then we come in,” she said. “And you let me finish what I didn’t get to say when the voice stopped.”
Rio muttered,
“And you make it up without a song and a vanishing act.” Curtis unlocked the door.
On a step that had been holding two girls instead of a courier request, Curtis Nightstar came home without a coat and found the loop
had walked itself to his building.
Anri Sonohara had pieces and an unfinished sentence.
Rio Kamichika had an IOU and no map.
He still was not telling them the alley.
“Come inside. Both of you.”
They did.
Shoes in the genkan. The furnished quiet of a place Justice had picked and Curtis still had not finished unpacking. Rio already knew the shape of the couch. Anri took it in like a map she had only been given in pieces — books, a closed bedroom, a spare door that had never had her name on it.
Curtis went into the spare room without a speech.
He came back with two keys on simple rings and put one in Anri’s palm, one in Rio’s.
“There’s a lot to unpack.” He let that sit, then shook his head once. “Before I do that. Instead of standing outside and waiting — you both have keys. You can wait inside.” A short breath. “My apologies for making you both wait.”
Rio’s fingers closed around the metal like it might vanish if she checked the weight wrong.
“…You’re just handing these over.”
“Yeah.”
Anri stared at the key in her hand.
Not a hotel card. Not a maybe. A copy of a door she had only known as an address he sent after a disappearing afternoon.
“…Curtis-kun.” Soft. Overwhelmed in the careful way she did everything. “This is—”
“So you don’t freeze on the step next time,” he said. “That’s all it has to be tonight.”
He turned toward the closet like the next motion had already been decided on a landing with Celty’s name in his pocket.
The helmet came out first.
Black and white, sharp as an oni’s face. Horns. A single red circle where a mouth and eyes should have been. Red-tipped cords hanging from the back like a second set of braids. The same sealed thing that had spoken through a voice module on a night neither girl was supposed to stack against this living room.
Curtis set it against his hip.
Anri went still.
The filtered voice arrived without sound. It’s okay. Let’s get out of here. A white helmet that had not been plastic. Please promise me you’ll be safe going home from here. The same sentence, later, from a boy in a Raira uniform who paid for a rideshare and slipped a number into a hug.
She did not say the blade.
She did not say the yellow helmet in a gutter.
She did not say she had cut someone whose name now sat in the same city as this apartment.
Her fingers tightened on the key until the ring bit.
“…That’s—” The word thinned. “That’s the one. From the street.”
Rio looked at the helmet, then at Anri, then at Curtis.
No surprise. Only confirmation.
“Yeah.” Rio’s voice was flat in the way of someone who had already ridden behind the red circle. “He takes that one when he drives. I sat on the bike. He made me a white one out of nothing and then put that on his own face like it was a seatbelt.” She glanced at Anri’s expression and softened a fraction. “You didn’t know it lived in his closet.”
Anri shook her head once.
The pieces were stacking whether he briefed them or not. Wanderer. Class rep. The rider who asked for nothing. The boy who thought a crowd had called him a freak.
Curtis’s thumb rested on the visor’s edge.
“I’m going to change.” Not a briefing. Not the alley. Not Mika. Not a hit. “You can sit. Kettle’s there. Spare room’s empty if you want a door that isn’t mine.”
Anri still had not looked away from the mask.
Rio sat on the couch she already knew, key in her fist, watching the girl who had pieces finally see the night version without a voice changer in the way.
On a furnished floor that had just grown two extra keys,
Curtis Nightstar put the Tech Rider on the table without putting the night in their mouths.
Rio Kamichika was not surprised.
Anri Sonohara recognized a promise she had heard
through red glass — and kept the reason she had been running behind her teeth.
The bedroom door clicked shut.
Water ran. A closet slid. The living room was left with a kettle, two keys, and a helmet-shaped hole in the air where he had been standing.
Rio turned on the couch.
“Wait. You didn’t know Curtis is the fabled Tech Rider people see around?”
Anri’s hands were still around the key ring. She shook her head once.
“No. I didn’t. No one does.” A small breath. “Rio-chan, you’re the only one that knows, I think.”
Rio blinked.
“…You’re kidding.”
“I’m not.” Anri’s voice stayed soft. Careful. The alley stayed behind her teeth. “There was a rider. A red visor. He helped me one night and asked me to get home safe. He didn’t give a name. I didn’t have a face.” Her ears warmed. “I thought— later I thought the words were the same. I didn’t have this.” She tipped her chin at the closed bedroom. “I didn’t have a closet.”
Rio leaned back, processing.
“I had the bike. The white helmet. He rode me home and talked through the speakers like a different person. I still knew it was him. Braids. Height. The way he says okay when he’s done arguing with himself.” She glanced at Anri. “I didn’t put it on a forum. I didn’t tell the class. I just… had it.”
Anri nodded.
“Then it stays here. Not a rumor. Not a crowd.” Her fingers tightened. “He already thinks people stare at him like a freak. If the school gets a mask with his name under it—”
“I know.” Rio’s mouth pulled. “I’m not posting your boyfriend.”
“He’s not—”
Rio’s look said fine and did not let go of the word.
The bedroom stayed closed.
Rio’s phone buzzed against her thigh. Hard. Not a single text. The kind of roll that meant a thread waking up all at once.
She almost ignored it.
Then she saw the banner.
Dollars.
Meeting.
Now.
The color she had joined because a house could be full and still leave a person with nowhere to stand. The same color Mikado Ryuugamine had built as a story and the city had decided was a gang. The same color that, tonight, had a first-year on a broken floor telling a headless woman and an info broker to watch him shine.
Rio’s face changed.
Anri saw it.
“…What.”
Rio locked the screen halfway, then didn’t bother. Anri was already close enough.
“Dollars meeting,” Rio said, quiet, like the apartment might report her. “Group call. Location drop. Everyone who lurks and everyone who actually shows.” A swallow. “I— I’m in it. I have been. I didn’t tell him. I didn’t tell you. It was just… people. A place that wasn’t my parents.”
Anri’s eyes widened a fraction.
“You’re—”
“A member. Not a leader. Not a color with a scarf. Just a handle on a site.” Rio’s thumb hovered. The thread kept stacking. Names she only knew as text. A time. A tone that was not the usual junk about urban legends. “This isn’t a meme post. This is a summons.”
From behind the bedroom door: a zipper. The soft knock of a helmet against a shelf.
Rio looked at the closed wood, then at Anri.
“He doesn’t know I’m one of them.” A thinner beat. “And I didn’t know he was the rider in the rumors. So I guess we’re even for five seconds.”
Anri’s key sat cold in her palm.
“Are you going.”
Rio stared at the screen.
The Dollars were waking up because someone in a one-room had logged in like a founder.
She did not have that piece.
She only had a meeting.
“I don’t know,” she said. “If I leave, he thinks I’m walking out of the IOU. If I stay, I miss whatever the city just decided to be.”
The phone buzzed again.
Attend if you can.
Ikebukuro. Tonight.
On a couch that had just learned two secrets, Rio Kamichika found out the color had called her name.
Anri Sonohara learned the girl who hated Izaya’s mouth already belonged to a crowd with no faces.
Behind a bedroom door, Curtis Nightstar put on a mask neither of them were supposed
to connect — and the Dollars opened without him.
The bedroom door opened.
Curtis came out already dressed for the version of himself that left the building after dark — red Jumpman cap pulled low, long black braids neat down the white shirt, red tie, black suspenders, the gray jacket with the red straps hanging open like he had not decided whether the night was a ride or a walk. Helmet still in the other room. Face visible. Phone in his hand like it had offended him.
He waved the screen once, dry.
“Lovely. All of a sudden a Dollars meeting.”
Both girls looked at him.
Rio’s phone was still face-down on her thigh, buzzing itself quiet. The same banner. The same summons. The same color she had just confessed on his couch.
Anri looked at the outfit first — host-sharp, street-ready, the man who handed out keys and then put a demon helmet on a shelf — and then at the word in his mouth.
Dollars.
Rio found her voice first. It came out too thin.
“…You got that.”
Curtis glanced up from the glass. Mismatched green and blue sat under the red brim like the cap was doing work a mask usually did.
“Yeah. Chat woke up like somebody kicked a hive.” He thumbed the thread without turning it toward them. Hanma’s handle stayed his. “I lurk. I don’t march. Tonight everybody’s marching.”
Rio’s key bit her palm.
He lurked.
He had the same meeting she did.
He did not look like a boy who had just learned the color existed. He looked like someone who had been in the room with the rumor and never raised his hand.
Anri’s voice stayed soft.
“You’re… in it.”
“I’m in a chat.” Curtis shrugged the jacket straight. “That’s not the same as wearing a color. Kadota’s people already tried to make it a membership. I said no.” His eyes flicked from Anri to Rio, reading the silence the way he read a landing. “You two look like I said a funeral instead of a forum.”
Rio swallowed.
The confession she had given Anri sat in the air, unspent, waiting to see if he would clock it without being told.
Anri looked at Rio. Then at him.
She did not out her.
She only said, careful,
“A lot of people are going to be in the same place tonight.”
Curtis’s mouth ticked.
“Yeah. That’s why it’s a bad night to have a face.” He tipped his head toward the bedroom, toward the oni helmet that Anri had recognized and Rio had already ridden behind. “Meeting can wait until I decide if I’m walking in as me or as a rumor.”
Rio stared at the phone in his hand like it had become a second key.
He had the summons.
He had the mask.
He still did not know she had been Dollars longer than she had been sitting on his couch.
On a furnished floor with two extra keys,
Curtis Nightstar walked out under a red cap and named the color like weather.
Rio Kamichika heard her own meeting in his mouth.
Anri Sonohara watched the chat, the jacket, and the girl who had not yet said me too.
The hive was awake. Nobody in the room had told the whole truth.
Curtis turned the phone face-down against his palm.
“Well now. Can’t have you both there.” He looked at Rio, then Anri, the red cap cutting a hard line over mismatched eyes. “So you should both go home. Or you can stay here. Either way, I don’t think it’s a good idea for either of you to go.”
A short beat. The jacket straps sat still.
“I got, oddly, a bad feeling about this. Like the day when Justice and I got captured by the cartel.”
The flat went quiet in a way tea could not fix.
Anri’s fingers tightened on the key until the ring left a mark.
“…Captured.” She did not ask for the map. She had already been given forests and scars and a brother who did not come back. A cartel was another door she was not going to open on his living-room floor. “You’re saying the meeting isn’t a meeting.”
“I’m saying crowds that wake up all at once stop being chats.” Curtis’s voice stayed flat. “Somebody kicked it. I can guess who. I can guess why. I’m not putting either of you in the splash.”
Rio had not moved.
Her phone was still buzzing itself out against her leg. Attend if you can. Ikebukuro. Tonight. The color she had joined so she would not only exist in a house that pretended nothing was wrong.
He did not know she was in it.
He was telling her to stay home like she was only a girl on his step with an IOU.
“…You lurk,” she said, careful. “And you still think it’s that bad.”
“I lurk so I can hear the weather before I walk into it.” His mouth ticked, no humor. “The last time a whole group of people got told to show up in one place, Justice and I thought we were just passing through. We weren’t. I’m not doing a second version of that story with you two in the van.”
Anri looked at the closed bedroom. At the helmet. At him.
“Then you shouldn’t go either.”
“That’s different.”
“It isn’t.” Soft. Stubborn. The unpaid sentence from the sidewalk still sitting in her chest. “You always make it different when it’s your body.”
Rio’s jaw worked.
Home meant her parents and a meeting she would miss and a boy who kept sending her away from the expensive parts of the city.
Stay meant his couch, his key, and the sound of a color calling her name from a pocket she had not emptied.
Go meant lying to the face that had just compared a forum ping to a cage.
She did not confess.
Not yet.
“…You’re not asking,” she said. “You’re telling.”
Curtis nodded once.
“Yeah. I am. Home or here. Not the meeting.” He flicked a glance at both of them. “If that feels like a cage, I’m sorry. I’d rather you be mad in my kitchen than missing in a crowd I can’t see the edges of.”
Anri swallowed.
“I’ll stay.” It came out small and immediate. “If you’re going out in that mask, someone should be here when you come back.”
Rio stared at her phone like it might forgive her for silencing it.
The Dollars kept stacking messages without her.
On a furnished floor that had just grown rules,
Curtis Nightstar named a cartel and a brother and a feeling
that did not belong on a forum.
Anri Sonohara chose the apartment.
Rio Kamichika held a summons he still thought was only his.
Rio’s phone went dark in her pocket.
“I’ll stay with Anri-chan.”
Curtis’s shoulders dropped a fraction.
“Thank you both.”
The red cap came off in one throw. It hit the back of the couch and sat there like a discarded civilian. Black braids fell forward against the white shirt. Heterochromia uncovered. The night version minus the seal.
Rio crossed the space before she could talk herself out of it and hooked her arms around him, face against the gray jacket, fists in the red straps.
“Come back to us, you giant bastard.”
It was not soft. It was not a rooftop speech. It was an IOU collected in advance.
Curtis went still the way he did when people put warmth on him without a receipt. Then one hand found her shoulder, brief, careful, like he was allowed to hold the line and not the whole girl.
Anri watched from the other side of the keys.
She knew that grip. She knew the way Rio said us like the apartment had already become a third place that was not a house and not a rumor. Something in her chest that had been competing since a classroom and a milk tea and a stare at a street fight finally sat down.
She was tired of fighting it.
She loved him. That was the plain version. She was not going to make him stand in his own genkan and choose a name. Rio was the exception — the girl he had pulled off a roof before any of them had a key. Anyone else would have been complicated. This was just two people who had both been caught by the same pair of hands.
Anri smiled. Small. Unarmed.
Rio let go like the hug had a time limit she would not overstay.
Curtis turned, picked up the helmet, and put the city over his face.
Black and white oni. Horns. One red circle. The decorative cords fell where his real braids had been. White smoke slipped along his collar — quick, private, the same trick Anri had felt in an alley and Rio had watched make a helmet out of air — and the true plaits vanished under jacket and seal. He tapped the module at the jaw once. The next breath came out cooler. Not quite him. Close enough to be a rumor.
“Something is going down.” The filtered voice filled the furnished quiet. “It’s best I don’t lie. I am to attend this meeting and make sure it goes off without a hitch.”
A beat.
The red visor turned toward Anri.
“If all works out… Anri-chan. Mika will come back to school. You will see her again.”
Anri’s breath caught.
The name she had not been allowed to say on a sidewalk. The girl she had used and been used by. The absence Seiji had walked out of class for. The someone Curtis had found and asked her not to turn into a crowd.
“…Mika.” It left her like a prayer she did not trust yet. “You— you still have her. She’s—”
“She’s alive.” The module kept it flat. Kind underneath. “That’s the piece you get tonight. The rest stays off this floor until the hitch is handled.”
Rio stood with her arms empty and the name Mika newly sitting in a room she had only owned as tea and a motorcycle ride.
She did not ask. Not after cartel. Not after stay.
Anri nodded once, eyes bright, the smile from a second ago shaking and holding anyway.
“Then come back,” she said, to the visor this time. The same request as a promise he had once asked of her. “We’ll be here.”
The Tech Rider’s head tipped.
Then he was moving — keys already theirs, hat on the couch, face gone, a meeting kicking in a city that did not know which handle was walking toward it.
On a furnished floor that had chosen to wait, Rio Kamichika held him
and called him a bastard like it was love
with the manners filed off.
Anri Sonohara stopped competing and kept a name she had missed for weeks.
Curtis Nightstar sealed the red circle, tucked the forest out of sight, and told one true thing
before the Dollars opened.
